      "answer": "$m=1$ at $(6,-3)$; $m=-7$ at $(-\\frac65,-\\frac35)$",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$(x-3)^2+y^2=18$, $y=mx-9$, leading to $(x-3)^2+(mx-9)^2=18$. M1. Finding equation of tangent and substituting into circle equation. Must be $mx-9$.\nExpand to $(m^2+1)x^2-(6+18m)x+72[=0]$. M1. Brackets expanded and all terms collected on one side. May be implied in discriminant. $m$ cannot be numeric.\n$(6+18m)^2-4(m^2+1)\\times72[=0]$. *M1. Use of $b^2-4ac$. Not in quadratic formula. $m$ cannot be numeric; $c$ must be numeric.\n$36m^2+216m-252[=0]$ leading to $m^2+6m-7=0$. DM1. Simplifies to 3-term quadratic.\n$m=1$ or $m=-7$. A1. Condone no method for solving quadratic shown.\n$m=1$ leading to $2x^2-24x+72=0$ leading to $x=6$. DM1. Must be correct $x$ for their quadratic.\n$m=-7$ leading to $50x^2+120x+72=0$ leading to $x=-\\frac65$. DM1. Must be correct $x$ for their quadratic.\n$(6,-3)$, $(-\\frac65,-\\frac35)$. A1.\nAlternative 1 for first 4 marks: use perpendicular distance formula $\\frac{|3m-1(0)-9|}{\\sqrt{m^2+1}}$ (M1); equate to $\\sqrt{18}$ (M1); square to $(3m-9)^2=18(m^2+1)$ (M1); obtain $9m^2-54m+81=0$ leading to $m^2+6m-7=0$ (M1).\nAlternative 2 for first 3 marks: differentiate implicitly or otherwise and equate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ to $m$, $(3-x)(9+6x-x^2)^{-1/2}=m$. (M1, OE); obtain $(1+m^2)x^2-6(1+m^2)x+9(1-m^2)[=0]$. (M1); discriminant $36(1+m^2)^2-4(1+m^2)\\times9(1-m^2)[=0]$. (M1).\nTotal: 8",
